{
  "requirement": "`OutlineItem::span` covers the item and every attachment it owns, including\nleading and trailing attachments. Selecting the item returns the contiguous\nsource slice covered by that span.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has two obligations. (1) `OutlineItem::span` covers the item and every attachment it owns, leading and trailing. (2) Selecting the item returns the contiguous source slice covered by that span. The reviewed tests cover both.\n\n- **Leading and trailing comments, Rust:** `src/agent/project_sources.rs#mod[tests]/fn[search_in_leading_and_trailing_comments_selects_the_function]` uses the source `// note before\\npub fn check() {} // note after\\nfn next() {}`. It selects `fn[check]` and asserts the text is exactly `// note before\\npub fn check() {} // note after` (after `trim_end`). That one assertion shows the selection spans a leading and a trailing comment and excludes the next item. It also checks that the header span is still the bare declaration, so the item's own spans are not widened.\n- **Trailing and leading comments, YAML:** `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[yaml_selection_includes_comments_owned_by_the_entry]` asserts these results:\n  - `key: value # trailing comment` is selected whole.\n  - The item's `span` equals `Span::new(0, text.len())`.\n  - `pair[d]` selects `# before next\\nd: 2`, which is a leading comment.\n  - `pair[a]` selects only `a:\\n  b: 1`, so the next entry's comment is not absorbed.\n- **Annotation attachments, Python:** `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[python_selected_source_includes_its_decorators]` asserts these results:\n  - The selected text of the decorated `def` equals the whole source, decorator included.\n  - `items[1].span.start == 0`.\n  - Two same-named decorated classes each select text beginning with their own decorator.\n- **Span contains attachments, custom language:** `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[custom_attachment_spans_are_checked_against_the_original_snapshot]` tests the span invariant with a custom `OutlineLang`. A valid attachment is accepted. Attachments that start before the item's span (`0..3`) or extend past it (`1..4`) are rejected, as are ones outside the snapshot or splitting a character. This enforces that attachments lie within the item span. It does not test that the span grows to cover attachments, but the Rust, YAML and Python tests do.\n\nThe tests cover leading and trailing attachments, both attachment kinds (comments and annotations), more than one language, and both the span value and the selected slice. Adding a leading or trailing attachment without extending the span would make these assertions fail. I found no unassessed obligation and no marking gap.",
